WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.137
Welcome to Module 5, Reasoning with SQL Logic.

00:00:04.137 --> 00:00:09.502
In the earlier modules, we focused on retrieving data, changing data safely, and creating summaries.

00:00:09.502 --> 00:00:17.892
Now we are moving into something that looks simple at first, but becomes very important in real projects:

00:00:17.892 --> 00:00:22.727
deciding which rows qualify and how each row should be interpreted.

00:00:22.727 --> 00:00:24.937
Think about a real system.

00:00:24.937 --> 00:00:32.232
A hospital may need to identify patients who are active, have given consent, have a high-risk diagnosis,

00:00:32.232 --> 00:00:35.282
and do not have usable contact information.

00:00:35.282 --> 00:00:40.792
A university may need to find students who are enrolled, have not completed a requirement,

00:00:40.792 --> 00:00:44.072
and either have a low grade or a missing assignment.

00:00:44.072 --> 00:00:54.272
A business may want customers who are active, have not purchased recently, and are eligible for a promotion.

00:00:54.272 --> 00:00:55.927
These are not just filtering problems.

00:00:55.927 --> 00:01:00.277
They are decision rules. In this module, we will work with AND, OR, and NOT,

00:01:00.277 --> 00:01:04.827
but we will also go deeper into SQL's treatment of missing values.

00:01:04.827 --> 00:01:10.387
SQL does not always produce only true or false.

00:01:10.387 --> 00:01:13.217
When NULL is involved, a condition can become UNKNOWN.

00:01:13.217 --> 00:01:18.007
That one idea explains many SQL results that surprise beginners.

00:01:18.007 --> 00:01:24.282
We will also use tools such as IS NULL, IS DISTINCT FROM, COALESCE, NULLIF, and CASE.

00:01:24.282 --> 00:01:28.752
The goal is not to memorize a list of keywords.

00:01:28.752 --> 00:01:32.747
The goal is to develop a reasoning process.

00:01:32.747 --> 00:01:34.612
Before writing SQL, state the rule in plain language.

00:01:34.612 --> 00:01:39.232
Decide what missing data means.

00:01:39.232 --> 00:01:40.822
Then write the condition and test edge cases.

00:01:40.822 --> 00:01:46.782
If you can explain why every row was included, excluded, or classified,

00:01:46.782 --> 00:01:52.999
then your SQL is doing more than running correctly; it is representing the rule correctly.
